name        star-burxt
version     0.1.0

# **No package dependency on BMX yet, and the reason is worth recording rather than rediscovering.**
#
# While `bmx.bx` still lives in Burxt's `lib/`, it IS part of the installed standard library, so
# `use "std/bmx.bx"` reaches it — no manifest entry required.
#
# The first attempt declared `dependency bmx <path-to-lib>`, which fails in a way that looks like a
# permissions problem and is not: a path dependency names a DIRECTORY, and `lib/` holds all
# twenty-seven modules. So `os_arg` and `file_read_maybe` were reported as "declared in the package
# `bmx`" — the whole standard library had been declared to be BMX. The same file also arrives twice,
# once through `std/` and once through the package, which is its own error.
#
# A path dependency wants a directory that is the package, and BMX now has one.
#
# **A COMMIT, NOT A TAG, and that is a decision rather than a gap.** `burxt.lock` pins commits
# rather than tags for the reason that applies here one level up: a tag can be moved and a commit
# cannot. BMX's Burxt half cannot be checked against any released compiler — `std/` is unreleased —
# so there is no green run behind a version number yet, and minting one would name something nothing
# has verified. It gets a tag when a run stands behind it.
#
# The import is `use "bmx/burxt/bmx.bx"` rather than `bmx/bmx.bx`: in the format's repository
# `reference/bmx.js` defines what BMX means and `burxt/bmx.bx` is one implementation of it. Putting
# the Burxt file at that repo's root would shorten this by seven characters and make a specification
# repository look like a Burxt package, which is the one thing it must not look like. It needs no
# manifest of its own — nothing reads a dependency's — so it stays exactly what it is.
dependency  bmx  https://github.com/andrecorugda/bmx  d06722a
